   Total cpu time =   1 hours, 18 minutes, 51 seconds
   Total wall time = 20 minutes, 38 seconds
   PROGRAM STOP => Normal termination.


The kinetic run is substantially more expensive than the ideal run (~20 minutes wall time vs. ~5 seconds), reflecting the parallel computation of the kinetic matrices over radial grid points and bounce-averaged integrals. The progress bar confirms the kinetic matrix was built successfully using 4 OpenMP threads.

The ``Finding kinetically displaced singular surfaces`` search attempts to locate kinetically shifted resonances but reports large residuals at several candidate psi values and ultimately finds no kinetic singular surfaces. This is typical for a stable, moderately rotating plasma where the kinetic corrections shift no rational surface determinant root across zero.

Comparing the two runs reveals the effect of kinetic corrections on delta-W:

+------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
| Quantity                     | Ideal (Run 1)    | Kinetic (Run 2)  |
+==============================+==================+==================+
| Plasma energy                | -9.238E-01       | -5.645E-01       |
+------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
| Vacuum energy                |  2.161E+00       |  2.005E+00       |
+------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
| Total (real)                 |  1.237E+00       |  1.440E+00       |
+------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
| Total (imaginary)            |  4.359E-05       | -4.161E-01       |
+------------------------------+------------------+------------------+

The kinetic plasma energy is less negative than the ideal value, indicating that finite-Larmor-radius and wave-particle resonance effects provide a net stabilising contribution to the plasma drive. The real total energy increases from 1.237E+00 to 1.440E+00, consistent with this additional stabilisation. The non-negligible imaginary component (-4.161E-01) in the kinetic run is characteristic of the kinetic Euler-Lagrange equation: it encodes the energy exchange between the MHD mode and the resonant particle population (i.e. wave-particle damping or drive), and its magnitude relative to the real part is a useful indicator of the strength of kinetic-resonant coupling.
